er=false, source=\"input_text\".\n- If a duration is NOT mentioned, do NOT invent a realistic value. Use exactly:\n  distribution=\"fixed\", value=1.0, unit=\"minutes\", is_placeholder=true, source=\"default_missing_duration\".\n- Apply this rule to every task duration AND to inter_arrival_time.\n- Never invent branch probabilities: include branch_probabilities only if stated in the text; otherwise leave them null and put the decision labels (e.g. \"igen\"/\"nem\") into branch_conditions and the sequence-flow condition_name.\n\nStructural correctness (CRITICAL):\n- Every task and gateway MUST have at least one outgoing and one incoming sequence_flow (unless adjacent to a start/end event).\n- All sequence_flow source_id and target_id values MUST reference existing element IDs.\n- Do NOT leave dead ends or orphans.\n\nPools and message flows (IMPORTANT - do not skip):\n- Whenever the text names two or more distinct organisational units, departments, workshops, roles, or locations that hand work, documents, or material to each other, you MUST model each as a separate pool, and list every element that belongs to it in element_ids. Do not collapse a multi-participant process into a single flat pool.\n- Sequence flows must NEVER cross pool boundaries. Model every hand-over between pools as:\n  message END event in the sending pool -> message_flow -> message START event in the receiving pool.\n- Set trigger_type=\"message\" on those events and give the message a short message_name.\n- Only use a single pool (pools=[]) if the whole process is genuinely performed by one unit.\n\nEvents:\n- event_position is \"start\", \"intermediate\", or \"end\". Use \"intermediate\" for milestones inside a pool (e.g. a phase completed).\n- Use trigger_type=\"message\" only for events that send/receive something across pools.\n\nData perspective (IMPORTANT - do not skip):\n- data_objects: physical or informational ARTEFACTS that flow through the process - documents, drawings, programs, form